[center][size=5][b]Nozavolochen[/b][/size]
One shared host DLL for independent spec-driven runtime mods[/center]
[b]What it does[/b]

Nozavolochen (Korvax for "convergence") is a single `xinput9_1_0.dll` that hosts any number of mods. Each mod is a folder with a `mod.toml` describing pattern patches the loader applies at game boot. Mods that are static data only need no framework support at all.

On its own, Nozavolochen does nothing. It only provides the infrastructure for mods to hook into the game.

[b]Why[/b]

Nozavolochen exists to unify mod loading under a single DLL, reducing conflicts and simplifying the modding process. When I built [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/nomanssky/mods/4697]Bigger Item Grids and Smaller Inventory Slots[/url] I needed to include the functionality of [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/nomanssky/mods/4545]NMS BootSkip[/url] as only one DLL can load at a time, which motivated the creation of a shared host for multiple mods.

[b]Install[/b]

Install the archive as a [b]Root Folder[/b] mod (Amethyst: right-click the mod and select [b]Enable Root Folder install[/b]) or drop its contents into the game folder, so `xinput9_1_0.dll` lands in `Binaries/`. Then install any Nozavolochen mods normally; each declares this mod as a required dependency.

On Linux (Proton), add this line to the game's Launch Options:

[code]WINEDLLOVERRIDES="xinput9_1_0=n,b" %command%[/code]

After launch, `Binaries/nozavolochen.log` records per-mod results to help with debugging.

[b]Compatibility[/b]
[list]
[*]Pattern-scanned hooks against NMS.exe, built for Cosmos v7.04. A game update can shift the patterns, and the log will say which patch refused to apply instead of misbehaving[/*]
[*]Forward the 7 XInput exports to the system DLL, so controllers keep working with or without patches applied[/*]
[/list]

